{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789243516,"nonce":"wX8xdHNidyGHahHzg4J7Ny6+sL0=","title":"A List\u003cT\u003e field inside a struct is laid out as the 24-byte header, but list values are header addresses","message":"`convert_fields_of` converts a `List\u003cT\u003e` field with `convert_type`, which\ngives the growable list's header struct, so the field occupies 24 bytes\ninline. Every list value elsewhere is the address of a heap header\n(`Struct(List)`-typed HIR values travel as pointers, and a function\nreturns one by address). Storing such a value into the field writes the\n8-byte address at the field's offset and leaves the rest as it was; a\nlater Load of the field copies 24 bytes from that offset into a stack\nslot, so the \"list\" read back is the address, the next field and\ngarbage, and pushing into it reallocates with a garbage capacity.\n\nThe Python frontend sidesteps it by storing every heap-typed field\nboxed (`field_storage`), which costs a tag check on each read. The fix\nis a single representation: a growable list is `Ptr(Struct List)` in\nevery position, including struct fields, function returns and globals,\nand `list_header_type()` stops being the type of a list value.","files":null}]}